    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Syst??????me de cote ??????nerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="10" build="20288">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.10b20288</English>
                <French>v15.10b20288</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16" build="20288">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16b20288</English>
                <French>v1.16b20288</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="true" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The infiltration test has shown air leakage from the following house components:

- Receptacles

- Some windows (2nd floor)

- All exterior doors

- Furnace chimney



During the infiltration test walkthrough, we notice air leakage on the items listed above. Airtightness is very important to achieve energy savings.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>At the time of the assessment, the insulation of the exterior walls was R-12. We recommend upgrading the insulation level. You are eligible for $2.50/square inch for the addition of R-20+, and $ 3.50/square inch for R-30+. The insulation will help you to increase the airtightness of your home and contribute to energy savings.</mainWalls>
                <windows>We recommend the replacement of the windows by triple pane and Energy Star certified units with Lowe coating and Argon filled.</windows>
                <doors>We recommend the replacement of the wood doors with new energy star certified products.  

</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>The result of the infiltration test has shown that your house's natural air exchange is not enough. We recommend the installation of an HRV (Heat Recovery Ventilator) HVI (Home Ventilation Institute) and Energy Star certified, to ensure proper ventilation inside your home.</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Have you already thought about having a heat pump? This type of heating system can use up to 3 times less energy than a typical baseboard and needs electricity to operate. However, this kind of system can?????????t operate on temperature below -25 degrees Celsius, it then require a secondary system to maintain the heat. In this case, the furnace can ensure the heat and the ducting system can be used by the heat pump to supply heat in the entire building. You might be eligible to participate in the Heat Pumps pilot project, please follow the link below for more information;

https://yukon.ca/en/news/expanded-rebates-renewable-home-heating-now-available



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

DEPRESSURIZATION TEST:

The depressurization test performed as shown a high risk of combustion gas spillage. Therefore, we recommend verifying your carbon monoxide detectors regularly.



ELECTRICAL USAGE:

 Concerning your electrical usage, I?????????ve listed some solutions/problems that might be useful to help you find what seems to be causing the problem. The Following items might explain and help you to investigate :



1-      APPLIANCES/DEVICES: It is possible in some case that one of the appliances or devices use more energy than expected.



SOLUTION: Turn off your electrical panel and turn on one by one the breakers one by one and take a look at your electric meter to see if you notice a higher usage. This option might help you to aim at which one seems to drain Energy.

Proceed with the same strategy with the other brokers to verify if the consumption comes from another circuit.



2-      ELECTRICAL WIRES: In some cases, the wires come in contact with the electrical box (switch or plugs). In this case, the current will flow continuously, which can explain the higher usage. This situation can be caused by a natural movement of the house that has for effect to pull on the wires at the point of exposing the wires at the point of touching the metal box.



3-      ELECTRICAL USAGE MONITOR: Another option is to use an electricity usage monitor to verify the electrical usage of your appliances/devices and verify if they are similar, close, or higher than the specified by the manufacturer.



If the electrical meter runs faster than normal when you turn on the breaker of the appliances/devices or circuit, it might be an indicator. This procedure might help you to find what is causing the increase in your consumption and select the one that seems to be wrong.



I suggest starting with these suggestions and see if you can find something, hopefully, it will help you find the problem. The report gives you an estimated electrical usage, which you can compare with your electrical usage on your electric bills.





ETS PROGRAM:

For more information concerning the Electrical Thermal Storage Unit, please follow the link below:

http://yukonconservation.org/programs/energy/electric-thermal-storage#:~:text=Electric%20thermal%20storage%20(ETS)%20is,providing%20heat%20throughout%20the%20day 



</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
